      "id": "VW-9",
      "title": "SDK: T4 write-and-verify primitive for unload (firmware idle-at-base-weight short-circuit)",
      "priority": 8,
      "severity": "medium",
      "estimate": 4,
      "done_when": "Either (a) SDK unloadDevice() guarantees the cable is mechanically unloaded before resolving (poll a verification register or telemetry-frame field for N ms post-write), so a subsequent startGuidedLoad emits the countdown ceremony; OR (b) the firmware's idle-at-base-weight pre-trigger behavior is characterized end-to-end and documented as a known limitation with a recommended consumer-side workaround. Either way includes hardware repro of the short-circuit + the fix path validated on bench.",
      "status": "open",
      "tags": [
        "sdk",
        "bug",
        "guided-load",
        "firmware"
      ],
      "notes": "Observed 2026-05-13 hardware (handoff: 'Open blockers / decisions needed' in HANDOFF-2026-05-12-wave2-merges-and-comprehensive-get-state.md, then re-confirmed this session). When startGuidedLoad is called from a state where the cable is already mechanically loaded at the target weight pre-trigger, the firmware short-circuits armed→active in <1s with NO countdown/engaging phase. unloadDevice (Workout.STOP) — even though it cleanly disengages a freshly Workout.GO-loaded cable — does NOT drive the firmware out of the idle-at-base-weight state. This is documented in SDK PR #62 JSDoc as a known failure mode + workaround (call unloadDevice() before startGuidedLoad). But the workaround itself doesn't fix the failure mode — the firmware can be in the bad state without the SDK knowing. A real fix is a T4 write-and-verify pattern in the SDK: after writing Workout.STOP, poll a register or telemetry frame to confirm the cable has physically reset before resolving the promise. Audit context: per sources/deepdive-sdk-surface-complexity.md, the SDK has zero T4 primitives — every setter is fire-and-forget (T2) or poll-based (T5). This would be the first true write-and-verify. Needs hardware research first: identify which register / telemetry-frame field reflects mechanical-cable-load state (Bug 17 vendor-state-refresh experiments may have already mapped some of this), then prototype the poll loop.",
